<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<subsection xml:id="subsection-dedekind-completeness" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>
    Dedekind completeness
  </title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      So far, we have failed to meaningfully distinguish between the properties enjoyed by the rational number system <m> \Q </m> and the real number system <m> \R </m>. We now address this issue via the notion of <term> Dedekind completeness </term>. Informally, Dedekind completeness is defined in terms of the set of upper bounds of a subset which is bounded from above (or dually the set of lower bounds of a subset which is bounded from below).
    </p>
  </introduction>
  
  <definition xml:id="definition-supremum-infimum">
    <title>
      Supremum; infimum
    </title>
    
    <idx>
      supremum
    </idx>
    
    <idx>
      least upper bound
    </idx>
    
    <idx>
      infimum
    </idx>
    
    <idx>
      greatest lower bound
    </idx>
    
    <notation>
      <usage>
        <m> \sup \of{ U } </m>
      </usage>
      
      <description>
        <p>
          the supremum of <m> U </m>
        </p>
      </description>
    </notation>
    
    <notation>
      <usage>
        <m> \inf \of{ U } </m>
      </usage>
      
      <description>
        <p>
          the infimum of <m> U </m>
        </p>
      </description>
    </notation>
    
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m> U \subseteq X </m> be a subset of a partially ordered set <m> \of{ X , \preceq } </m>.
        
        <ul>
          <li>
            <title>
              Supremum
            </title>
            
            <p>
              If the set of upper bounds for <m> U </m> in <m> X </m> has a minimum, then this minimum is called the <term> supremum </term> (or <term> least upper bound </term>) of <m> U </m> and is denoted <m> \sup \of{ U } </m>; that is,
              
              <md>
                \sup \of{ U } = \min \of{ \setmid{ x \in X }{ u \leq x \textrm{ for all } u \in U } }
              </md>.
            </p>
          </li>
          
          <li>
            <title>
              Infimum
            </title>
            
            <p>
              If the set of lower bounds for <m> U </m> in <m> X </m> has a maximum, then this maximum is called the <term> infimum </term> (or <term> greatest lower bound </term>) of <m> U </m> and is denoted <m> \inf \of{ U } </m>; that is,
              
              <md>
                \inf \of{ U } = \max \of{ \setmid{ x \in X }{ x \leq u \textrm{ for all } u \in U } }
              </md>.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>
  
  <example xml:id="example-suprema-and-infima">
    <title>
      Suprema and infima
    </title>
    
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m> \of{ U_{ j } }_{ j \in \mathcal{ J } } </m> be an indexed family of subsets <m> U_{ j } \subseteq X </m> of a set <m> X </m>. These subsets <m> U_{ j } </m> are elements of the power set <m> \ps \of{ X } </m>, which is partially ordered by set containment <m> \subseteq </m>.
      </p>
      
      <p>
        We may reinterpret (a) of <xref ref="problem-indexed-intersections-and-unions"/> as saying that the indexed intersection <m> \bigcap_{ j \in \mathcal{ J } }{ U_{ j } } </m> and the indexed union <m> \bigcup_{ j \in \mathcal{ J } }{ U_{ j } } </m> are the infimum and supremum of <m> \set{ U_{ j } }_{ j \in \mathcal{ J } } </m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </example>

  <remark xml:id="remark-suprema-of-dedekind-cuts">
    <title>
      Suprema of Dedekind cuts
    </title>
    
    <statement>
      <p>
        The complement <m> \Q \setminus X </m> of a Dedekind cut <m> X \in \R </m> is exactly the set of upper bounds of <m> X </m> in <m> \Q </m>, and so <xref ref="lemma-rational-dedekind-cuts"/> implies that <m> X </m> is rational if and only if it has a supremum in <m> \Q </m>.
      </p>
      
      <p>
        This is the deficiency of the rational numbers <m> \Q </m> from the analytic perspective: <xref ref="proposition-irrational-dedekind-cuts"/> posits the existence of subsets of <m> \Q </m> which are bounded from above <alert> and yet do not have suprema </alert>. We will shortly see that this is not the case in the real number system <m> \R </m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </remark>
  
  <definition xml:id="definition-dedekind-completeness">
    <title>
      Dedekind completeness
    </title>
    
    <idx>
      Dedekind completeness
    </idx>
    
    <statement>
      <p>
        An ordered field <m> \of{ F , + , \cdot , \leq } </m> is called <term> Dedekind complete </term> if every non-empty subset of <m> F </m> which is bounded from above has a supremum.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>
  
  <theorem xml:id="theorem-dedekind-completeness-of-the-real-numbers">
    <title>
      Dedekind completeness of the real numbers
    </title>
    
    <statement>
      <p>
        The real number system <m> \of{ \R , + , \cdot , \leq } </m> is Dedekind complete.
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <proof>
      <p>
        Let <m> \emptyset \subsetneq U \subseteq \R </m> be a non-empty subset of <m> U </m> which is bounded from above, and consider the indexed union
        
        <md>
          S = \bigcup_{ X \in U }{ X } = \setmid{ q \in \Q }{ q \in X \textrm{ for some } X \in U }
        </md>.
        
        Since <m> U </m> is a non-empty collection of non-empty subsets of <m> \Q </m>, <m> S </m> is non-empty. Moreover, since <m> U </m> is bounded from above, there is some Dedekind cut <m> Y \in \R </m> so that <m> X \subseteq Y </m> for all <m> X \in U </m>. In particular, if <m> q \in \Q \setminus Y </m>, then <m> q \notin X </m> for all <m> X \in U </m>, and so <m> q \notin S </m>.
      </p>
      
      <p>
        Let <m> p , q \in \Q </m> be rational numbers, and suppose that <m> p \leq q </m> and <m> q \in S </m>. Since <m> q \in S </m>, <m> q \in X </m> for some <m> X \in U </m>, and so <m> p \in X \subseteq S </m>.
      </p>
      
      <p>
        Now let <m> p \in S </m>, so that <m> p \in X </m> for some <m> X \in U </m>. Since <m> X </m> does not have a maximum, there is some rational number <m> q \in X \subseteq S </m> so that <m> p \lt q </m>. We conclude that <m> S </m> does not have a maximum, and so is a Dedekind cut.
      </p>
      
      <p>
        We note that (a) of <xref ref="problem-indexed-intersections-and-unions"/> implies that for a Dedekind cut <m> Y \in \R </m>, <m> S \subseteq Y </m> if and only if <m> X \subseteq Y </m> for all <m> X \in U </m>; that is, any upper bound for <m> U </m> in <m> \R </m> contains <m> S </m>, so that
        
        <md>
          S = \min \of{ \setmid{ Y \in \R }{ X \subseteq Y \textrm{ for all } X \in U } } = \sup \of{ U }
        </md>.
      </p>
    </proof>
  </theorem>
  
  <p>
    We will see that <xref ref="theorem-dedekind-completeness-of-the-real-numbers"/> has far-reaching consequences for the discipline of real analysis, most notably in the existence of limits for certain objects constructed out of the real numbers <m> \R </m>. First, however, we present the following non-constructive characterization of <m> \R </m> as the <em> unique </em> Dedekind complete ordered field.
  </p>
  
  <theorem xml:id="theorem-uniqueness-of-the-real-numbers">
    <title>
      Uniqueness of the real numbers
    </title>
    
    <statement>
      <p>
        If <m> \of{ F , + , \cdot , \leq } </m> is a Dedekind complete ordered field, then there is a bijection <m> \varphi \colon \R \to F </m> so that for all real numbers <m> x , y \in \R </m>, <m> \varphi \of{ x + y } = \varphi \of{ x } + \varphi \of{ y } </m>, <m> \varphi \of{ x \cdot y } = \varphi \of{ x } \cdot \varphi \of{ y } </m>, and if <m> x \leq y </m>, then <m> \varphi \of{ x } \leq \varphi \of{ y } </m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </theorem>

  <conclusion>
    <p>
      With <xref ref="theorem-uniqueness-of-the-real-numbers"/>, we are now ready to shake off the burden of viewing the real number system <m> \R </m> through the perspective of Dedekind cuts. All relevant properties of the analysis of real numbers can be derived abstractly from the axioms of a Dedekind complete ordered field, and as a consequence, we will see that we no longer need to rely on any specific formal model of <m> \R </m>.
    </p>
  </conclusion>
</subsection>
